<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">General Strike of 1926</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1926">1926</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as 1926 general strike</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The General Strike of 1926 was a nine-day strike in the United Kingdom from 3 to 12 May 1926, called by the Trades Union Congress in support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> miners facing wage cuts and longer hours. About 1.7 million workers came out, but the government kept services running with volunteers and the TUC called off the strike, leaving the miners to fight on alone until they were defeated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/general-strike-of-1926.jpg" alt="General Strike of 1926" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tyldesley_miners_outside_the_Miners_Hall_during_the_1926_General_Strike.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Heavy wartime use had depleted British coal seams: output per miner fell from 310 tons a year in the early 1880s to 199 tons in 1920–1924. In 1924 the Dawes Plan let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> export coal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>, lowering prices, and in 1925 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a>, as chancellor of the Exchequer, returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to the gold standard, making exports dearer. Mine owners cut wages, and when they proposed further cuts the Miners' Federation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> answered: &quot;Not a penny off the pay, not a minute on the day.&quot;</p><p>Stanley Baldwin's Conservative government paid a nine-month subsidy and appointed a royal commission under Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-samuel">Herbert Samuel</a>. Its report of 10 March 1926 recommended reorganising the industry, cutting wages by 13.5% and ending the subsidy. The owners then offered longer hours and lower pay, which the miners refused, and they were locked out.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After talks failed, the TUC limited the strike to railwaymen, transport workers, printers, dockers, ironworkers and steelworkers. The government had prepared during the subsidy, stockpiling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, creating the Organisation for the Maintenance of Supplies and using the Emergency Powers Act 1920, while troops and volunteers kept basic services going. Churchill edited the government's British Gazette, and the TUC published the British Worker.</p><p>Police and strikers clashed in Canning Town, Poplar and Tilbury, and on 10 May striking miners derailed the Flying Scotsman at Cramlington; elsewhere, in Plymouth, strikers beat a police team 2–0 at football. Mr Justice Astbury ruled in the High Court that the general strike was not protected by the Trade Disputes Act 1906. On 12 May the TUC General Council called the strike off without any guarantee against victimisation of the strikers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The miners held out until most returned by the end of November, accepting longer hours, lower wages and district agreements, and many stayed unemployed for years. The Trade Disputes and Trade Unions Act 1927 banned sympathy strikes, general strikes and mass picketing. Union membership fell, and Ernest Bevin, who had coordinated the strike, came to see it as a mistake, while many rank-and-file workers felt betrayed by the TUC. Britain has had no further general strike. The strike appears in novels by H. G. Wells, John Galsworthy, Lewis Grassic Gibbon and Evelyn Waugh.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “1926 United Kingdom general strike” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Between the wars the old staple industries of coal, cotton, shipbuilding and iron declined, leaving mass unemployment in South Wales, the North of England, Clydeside and Northern Ireland; the <b>General Strike</b> of May 1926 and the Jarrow March of 1936 became symbols of the era.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
